  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good on the core 'do not invent semantics' pressure because payload stays opaque behind pCID dispatch and unknown handlers fail explicitly. It is only a partial fit for malformed UDP datagrams because the specimen does not define the UDP binding or how non-CBOR bad bytes are preserved, surfaced, and logged.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear byte/handler boundary: payload stays opaque until pCID-selected interpretation.",
      "Unknown pCID handling preserves exact envelope bytes and forbids speculative parsing.",
      "Deterministic CBOR and no central pCID registry assumption help long-lived, sparse-knowledge evolution."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No UDP transport binding is specified, so malformed-datagram pass-through is indirect rather than explicit.",
      "The spec does not define handling for invalid CBOR, truncation, or other non-envelope bytes.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Implementations may diverge at the raw-datagram parse boundary.",
      "Signature-wrapper layering may complicate interoperability and audit trails."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should a UDP-feed binding for this specimen surface exact datagram bytes upward even when CBOR decode fails?",
      "What peer-local record should distinguish malformed bytes, unsupported pCID, and bad wrapper signatures?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
